= Large language model =

== Overview ==
A '''large language model''' ('''LLM''') is a large-scale statistical or neural language model trained over very large corpora to model patterns in token sequences and commonly adapted to perform a wide range of language-related tasks.

Most contemporary LLMs are deep neural networks trained with self-supervised objectives and later adapted through instruction tuning, preference optimization, retrieval, tools, or other methods.

== Language modeling ==
A language model assigns probabilities to sequences or predicts portions of a sequence from context.

Autoregressive models commonly estimate:

<pre>
P(x_1, ..., x_n)
= Π_t P(x_t | x_<t)
</pre>

Other language-model objectives can predict masked or corrupted tokens rather than only the next token.

== What “large” means ==
There is no universal parameter-count threshold that makes a language model “large.”

The term reflects historical scale in parameters, training data, computation, and capability relative to earlier language models.

== Transformers ==
Most modern LLMs use Transformer architectures.

However:

<pre>
large language model ≠ transformer by definition
</pre>

Transformer describes an architecture family; LLM describes a scale/domain/model class.

== Foundation models ==
Many LLMs are [[Artificial intelligence|AI]] foundation models because broad pretraining allows adaptation to many downstream tasks.

But the concepts emphasize different properties:

<pre>
LLM              → large-scale language modeling
foundation model → broad pretraining + downstream adaptability
</pre>

Not every foundation model is a language model.

== Chatbots ==
A chatbot is an application/interface.

It may use an LLM, retrieval, safety layers, tools, memory, and orchestration.

Therefore:

<pre>
LLM ≠ chatbot
</pre>

== Agents ==
An [[AI agent]] can use an LLM for reasoning-like text generation, planning, tool selection, interpretation, or communication.

The model itself is not automatically an agent because it does not by itself define persistent goals, action interfaces, environmental feedback, or task orchestration.

== Capabilities and limitations ==
LLM behavior depends on:

* training data;
* architecture;
* scale;
* adaptation;
* context;
* prompting;
* retrieval;
* tools;
* decoding;
* system-level controls.

Fluent output does not guarantee factual correctness, formal reasoning validity, grounded understanding, or reliable long-horizon action.

